exports[`module federation snapshot > react host and remote (ts) 1`] = `
{
  "README.md": "# my-app

Micro-frontends with Module Federation, as two independent projects:

- \`host/\` (my-app-host): the app, on port 5173. It loads \`Counter\` from the remote at runtime.
- \`remote/\` (my-app-remote): exposes \`./Counter\`, on port 5174.

Each has its own dependencies and can live in its own repository and deploy on its own schedule.

## Getting started

Start the remote, then the host, in two terminals:

\`\`\`bash
cd remote && npm install && npm run dev
\`\`\`

\`\`\`bash
cd host && npm install && npm run dev
\`\`\`

Open http://localhost:5173. If the remote is not running, the host page stays blank.
",
  "host/.gitignore": "node_modules
dist
.env
.env.local
.DS_Store
",
  "host/README.md": "# my-app-host

Stack: React 19, Vite, TypeScript, Module Federation.

## Getting started

\`\`\`bash
npm install
npm run dev
\`\`\`

## Scripts

- \`npm run build\`
- \`npm run preview\`
- \`npm run typecheck\`

## Micro-frontends

This is the Module Federation host (port 5173). It loads \`Counter\` from the remote project at runtime (\`http://localhost:5174/remoteEntry.js\`), so start the remote first: if it is unreachable, this page stays blank. Before deploying, point the remote \`entry\` in vite.config at the remote's real URL.

## Push to GitHub

Create an empty repository on GitHub, then:

\`\`\`bash
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/<your-username>/my-app-host.git
git push -u origin main
\`\`\`
",
